The set of polynomials \(\{p_1(x), p_2(x), p_3(x), p_4(x)\}\) is linearly dependent because they belong to the vector space of polynomials of degree at most 2, which has dimension 3. Any set of 4 vectors in a 3-dimensional space must be linearly dependent.

To find a linearly independent subset, we represent the polynomials as coordinate vectors with respect to the standard basis \(\{1, x, x^2\}\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
p_1(x) = x^2 + 1 &\quad\Rightarrow\quad (1, 0, 1) \\
p_2(x) = x^2 + x &\quad\Rightarrow\quad (0, 1, 1) \\
p_3(x) = x + 2   &\quad\Rightarrow\quad (2, 1, 0) \\
p_4(x) = x^2 + 3 &\quad\Rightarrow\quad (3, 0, 1)
\end{aligned}
\]

Form a matrix with these vectors as columns and row-reduce to find the pivot columns:

\[
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\
0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
1 & 1 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{R_3 \leftarrow R_3 - R_1}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\
0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & -2 & -2
\end{bmatrix}
\xrightarrow{R_3 \leftarrow R_3 - R_2}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\
0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & -3 & -2
\end{bmatrix}
\]

The pivots are in columns 1, 2, and 3, so the first three vectors are linearly independent. Thus, one linearly independent subset is:

\[
\{p_1(x),\; p_2(x),\; p_3(x)\}
\]

